{"id":"R0001","access":"READ","pdf":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","anchor":{"page":2,"locator":"§II.D","quote":"The objective of this paper is to obtain model partition"},"identity":{"title":"Adaptive Partition and Resource Allocation for Collaborative Inference Services in UAV Swarm","authors":["Ying Dong","Jiebei Shi","Songwei Li","Yuzhou Gao","Yeping Zhu","Jianbing Zhang","Jiwei Huang"],"year":2026,"venue":"2026 IEEE International Conference on Web Services (ICWS)","doi":"10.1109/ICWS72778.2026.00154","locator":"physical PDF p. 1 header/footer"},"criteria":{"E2":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Simulation explicitly evaluates 10 UAVs, 4 edge servers, and 1 cloud server using a public drone delivery dataset and real base station locations.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","page":3,"locator":"§III.A","quote":"This experiment considers 10 UAVs, 4 ESs and one cloud","gap":null},"E3":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Partitions an L-layer DNN via two partition points p_k and q_k across UAV, edge server, and cloud, transferring intermediate features and jointly optimizing partitioning, UAV frequency, server compute, and bandwidth via PPO and KKT convex subproblems.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","page":2,"locator":"§II.B","quote":"transmitting intermediate inference results between ES and cloud server","gap":null},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Provides analytical KKT derivations and trace-driven simulation comparing CAPRI against 7 baselines (local, DQN, cloud_only, no_local, edge_only, end_edge, no_energy) on reward convergence and reward versus latency weight alpha, though DNN model identity, system parameters, and numeric latency/energy values are omitted.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0001.pdf","page":3,"locator":"§III.A","quote":"the reward convergence of all algorithms, where CAPRIC","gap":null},"E7":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"Published in IEEE ICWS 2026 proceedings (3 pages, pp. 1222-1224) as Work-in-Progress paper CWS_WIP_222; IEEE SERVICES 2026 states all submitted manuscripts are double-blind peer-reviewed by at least three PC members, but §III.A omits the evaluated DNN architecture, compute/radio parameters, numeric latency/energy metrics/units, and Fig. 2-3 plots did not render readable data in PDF text extraction.","source":"https://services.conferences.computer.org/2026/icws-program/","page":null,"locator":"CWS-WIP-S3; checked PDF §II-§III.A pp. 1-3","quote":"CWS_WIP_222: Adaptive Partition and Resource Allocation for Collaborative Inference","gap":"Work-in-Progress track paper (3 pages) with omitted DNN model, hardware/radio parameters, and numeric latency/energy metrics leaves §P03 E7(b) identifiable conditions and metrics unverified pending visual inspection of Fig. 2-3.","extra_evidence":[{"source":"https://services.conferences.computer.org/2026/info-for-authors/","page":null,"locator":"Information for Authors / Page limits","quote":"All submitted manuscripts will be peer-reviewed by at least three"}]}},"recommendation":"UNRESOLVED","primary_code":null,"author_check":"Inspect PDF p. 3 Fig. 2-3 plots visually and adjudicate whether 3-page ICWS 2026 WIP paper CWS_WIP_222 satisfies §P03 E7(b) identifiable evaluation conditions and metrics despite omitting DNN model and system parameters."}
{"id":"R0113","access":"READ","pdf":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","anchor":{"page":9,"locator":"IMPLEMENTATION AND RESULTS","quote":"Experimental evaluation was conducted using a simulated autonomous UAV swarm"},"identity":{"title":"A Neuromorphic Spiking Neural Network-Based Continual Learning Framework for Energy-Efficient Edge Intelligence in Autonomous UAV Swarm Navigation","authors":["Singareddy Hemasri","K.S. Raghavendra Reddy"],"year":2026,"venue":"American Journal of AI Cyber Computing Management","doi":"DOI_NOT_FOUND","locator":"physical PDF p. 1 and https://ajaccm.com/journal/index.php/ajaccm/article/view/771"},"criteria":{"E2":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Reports simulation evaluation with 40 cooperative UAVs and scalability tests across 10, 25, 50, and 100 UAVs.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":9,"locator":"IMPLEMENTATION AND RESULTS","quote":"40 cooperative UAVs operating within dynamic urban environments, forest terrains","gap":null},"E3":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"Each UAV runs onboard SNN perception/navigation locally without cloud computing, while prose asserts periodic exchange of compact navigation embeddings aggregated via graph message passing; however, no concrete SNN/GNN architecture, feature-transfer protocol, or neural fusion equations beyond a schematic linear-concatenation formula are defined.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":7,"locator":"METHODOLOGY; checked Abstract p. 1, Methodology pp. 5-8, Implementation pp. 9-13","quote":"Each UAV periodically exchanges compact navigation embeddings, local obstacle information","gap":"High-level prose asserts inter-UAV navigation embedding exchange and GNN coordination without specifying an identifiable collaborative neural inference workload, layer/feature representation, or operational fusion procedure."},"E4":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"Reports four summary tables and charts comparing CNN, Vision Transformer, Conventional SNN, and NSNN-CL across simulation and HIL claims, but omits dataset/map definitions, baseline architectures, metric formulas (e.g., Energy Efficiency %, Continual Learning Score %), and hardware specs, and Fig. 3 plots UAV counts and percentages up to 400 on one axis with default Series1-Series4 labels.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0113.pdf","page":10,"locator":"Table 1; checked Implementation and Results pp. 9-14, Tables 1-4, Figs. 1-4","quote":"Performance comparison of autonomous UAV swarm navigation using different","gap":"Identifiable experimental conditions, metric definitions, and verifiable evaluation of a collaborative inference method are not established."},"E7":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"Published as a 17-page article (pp. 448-464) in AJACCM, which self-declares peer review on its PDF header and About page, but the publisher website advertises 1-2 day acceptance and 1-3 day publication, lists an incomplete non-resolving DOI prefix (https://doi.org/10.64751/), and the paper lacks concrete method/evaluation specifications.","source":"https://ajaccm.com/journal/index.php/ajaccm/about","page":null,"locator":"About AJACCM; checked PDF pp. 1-17 and article page view/771","quote":"is a peer-reviewed, open-access scholarly journal dedicated to advancing research","gap":"Self-claimed peer review contradicts the publisher's advertised 1-2 day acceptance window and incomplete DOI prefix; independent verification of legitimate peer review and substantive completeness is lacking.","extra_evidence":[{"source":"https://ajaccm.com/journal/index.php/ajaccm/article/view/771","page":null,"locator":"How to Cite & Journal Information","quote":"American Journal of AI Cyber Computing Management, 6(1), 448-464."}]}},"recommendation":"UNRESOLVED","primary_code":null,"author_check":"Adjudicate E7, E3, and E4: decide whether AJACCM's 1-2 day acceptance claim and broken DOI fail E7 peer-review/completeness and whether R0113's unspecified SNN/GNN method and undefined metrics fail E3 and E4."}
{"id":"R0145","access":"READ","pdf":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","anchor":{"page":6,"locator":"§3.1","quote":"each UAV is equipped with a small LLM"},"identity":{"title":"LLM-based UAV Path Planning for Autonomous and Adaptive Industry Systems","authors":["Wenjing Xiao","Chenglong Shi","Miaojiang Chen","Athanasios V. Vasilakos","Min Chen","Ahmed Farouk"],"year":2025,"venue":"ACM Transactions on Autonomous and Adaptive Systems","doi":"10.1145/3774655","locator":"physical PDF p. 1 and p. 22"},"criteria":{"E2":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Simulation evaluates a swarm of 10 UAVs and 1 cloud server in a 20m x 20m grid across 20 dynamically generated scenarios.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":14,"locator":"§6.1","quote":"The UAV swarm consists of 10 UAVs, and their initial","gap":null},"E3":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"Deploys a 4-bit quantized small LLM (LLaMA-3.2-3B) on each UAV and a large LLM (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) on the cloud, assigning each UAV's waypoint-planning inference request to the cloud large LLM when connected or to its local small LLM when disconnected (hybrid LLM); however, each UAV acts solely on local observations with no inter-UAV collaboration, no layer splitting, and no joint orchestration solver for Eq. 14.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":6,"locator":"§3.1; checked §1 p. 2, §3.1-§3.5 pp. 6-9, §5.1 pp. 12-13, §6.2 pp. 16-18, §7 p. 20","quote":"the cloud-based large LLM then guides the UAV","gap":"Borderline E3 scope: explicit UAV-edge/cloud LLM inference request assignment exists via connectivity-triggered fallback, but inference serves per-UAV waypoint planning without inter-UAV collaboration or joint orchestration.","extra_evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":20,"locator":"§7","quote":"each UAV generates movement decisions based solely on local observations."}]},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Evaluates 7 fine-tuned LLMs and compares small LLM only, large LLM only, and hybrid LLM against DRL-USPP (DQN) and ACO-USPP across 20 scenarios (10 UAVs) and 1,000 decision samples on communication time, TTFT/TPOT inference time, GFLOPs, steps, and task completion.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":17,"locator":"§6.2","quote":"they are large LLM only, hybrid LLM, and small LLM","gap":null},"E7":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Full 22-page journal article (Just Accepted version, received 23 June 2025, revised 6 September 2025, accepted 22 October 2025) in ACM Transactions on Autonomous and Adaptive Systems; ACM policy mandates that all ACM Transactions are refereed journals reviewed by at least three qualified independent reviewers.","source":"https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/pre-publication-evaluation","page":null,"locator":"Categories of publications / Refereeing","quote":"Submitted papers shall be reviewed by at least three qualified","gap":null,"extra_evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0145.pdf","page":22,"locator":"Article history footer","quote":"Received 23 June 2025; revised 6 September 2025; accepted 22"}]}},"recommendation":"UNRESOLVED","primary_code":null,"author_check":"Adjudicate E3: decide whether connectivity-triggered per-UAV cloud-large versus onboard-small LLM waypoint-planning switching (without inter-UAV collaboration or joint orchestration) qualifies as collaborative inference."}
{"id":"R0223","access":"READ","pdf":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","anchor":{"page":3,"locator":"§3.2","quote":"captures RGB-D images and feeds them into the Grounding"},"identity":{"title":"Outdoor Multi-UAV Collaborative Task Planning System Based on Multi-Agent and Improved PPO Algorithm","authors":["Runmin Wang","Zhongliang Deng"],"year":2025,"venue":"2025 5th International Conference on Automation Control, Algorithm and Intelligent Bionics (ACAIB 2025)","doi":"10.1145/3760269.3760299","locator":"physical PDF p. 1 ACM Reference Format"},"criteria":{"E2":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Evaluates 3-member and 6-member multi-UAV path planning in MATLAB (6 and 9 obstacles) and multi-UAV cooperative task planning in AirSim Wind Farm and Residential Courtyard scenes.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":6,"locator":"Fig. 5 caption","quote":"the 3D path simulation of a 6-agent scenario","gap":null},"E3":{"status":"NOT_MET","finding":"Uses a central server LLM (Doubao-1.5-pro) for natural-language task decomposition/scheduling (UTTUT and P3 factor graph), CTDE PPO+ for multi-UAV trajectory planning, and standalone on-drone Grounding DINO detection where only the first reporting UAV's target coordinates are selected; §4.2/Fig. 7 compares static task-planning architectures (Centralization, Parallelism, Distribution, Master) without neural partitioning, multi-UAV feature fusion, or collaborative inference orchestration.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":4,"locator":"§3.2; checked §1 p. 1, §3.1-§3.2 pp. 2-4, §4.1-§4.2 pp. 5-7, Fig. 7 p. 7","quote":"the system selects the feedback from the first agent to report","gap":null},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Provides MATLAB path-planning comparisons against PPO, DDPG, and SAC (Table 1) and AirSim cooperative task-planning experiments (>=30 trials per task) across four architectures on success rate, R-Time, and C-Time (Table 2), though evaluating task/path planning rather than collaborative neural inference.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0223.pdf","page":5,"locator":"§4.2","quote":"distribution, and master frameworks shown in Figure 7 to assess","gap":null},"E7":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Full 8-page conference paper (pp. 193-200) in ACAIB 2025 published in ACM ICPS (ISBN 979-8-4007-1431-3/2025/06); ACAIB's official editorial policy documents double-blind peer review by 2-3 independent expert reviewers and ACM ICPS policy mandates formal peer-review management and archiving.","source":"https://www.acaib.org/EditorialPolicy","page":null,"locator":"Editorial Policy Step 3","quote":"reviewed by at least two or three independent expert reviewers","gap":null,"extra_evidence":[{"source":"https://www.acm.org/publications/icps/publication-policy","page":null,"locator":"ICPS Conference Publication Policy","quote":"Manage the review process in a timely, confidential, and appropriate"}]}},"recommendation":"EXCLUDE","primary_code":"E3","author_check":"Confirm E3 exclusion: verify that R0223's LLM task planning, MARL PPO trajectory planning, standalone first-to-report Grounding DINO perception, and static §4.2/Fig. 7 architecture comparison do not constitute collaborative neural inference."}
{"id":"R0292","access":"READ","pdf":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","anchor":{"page":3,"locator":"§4","quote":"The proposed system comprises two quadrotor UAVs, D1 and"},"identity":{"title":"Continuous Marine Tracking via Autonomous UAV Handoff","authors":["Heegyeong Kim","Alice James","Avishkar Seth","Endrowednes Kuantama","Jane Williamson","Yimeng Feng","Richard Han"],"year":2025,"venue":"MobiSys '25 (DroNet 2025 Workshop)","doi":"10.1145/3711875.3737661","locator":"physical PDF p. 1 and https://dblp.org/db/conf/mobisys/mobisys2025"},"criteria":{"E2":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Defines a two-quadrotor UAV system (D1 and D2) and evaluates D1-to-D2 target-template handover matching plus a 180 s HIL test including inter-UAV coordination, although full two-drone outdoor flight handoff is not reported.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":3,"locator":"§4","quote":"The proposed system comprises two quadrotor UAVs, D1 and","gap":null},"E3":{"status":"NOT_MET","finding":"Each UAV independently executes standalone single-object neural tracking (OSTrack on Jetson Nano); upon low battery, D1 hands off tracking duty sequentially to D2 by transferring a cropped RGB target template matched via classical non-neural ORB keypoints and ArUco marker pose estimation, without neural partitioning, feature fusion, or collaborative inference orchestration.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":3,"locator":"§4; checked §1 pp. 1-2, §3.3 p. 3, §4-§4.1 pp. 3-4, §5.1-§5.2 pp. 4-6","quote":"Each UAV independently runs the most effective","gap":null,"extra_evidence":[{"source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":3,"locator":"§3.3","quote":"ORB is a fast and lightweight"}]},"E4":{"status":"SUPPORTED","finding":"Evaluates 5 visual trackers offline on 5,200 shark video frames (Tables 1-2), D1-to-D2 ORB template-matching target coverage across 30/50/70 px paddings (Table 3), and 180 s HIL onboard Jetson Nano CPU, GPU, memory, and power (Fig. 5), though without end-to-end two-UAV flight handoff metrics.","source":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","page":5,"locator":"§5.2","quote":"feature matching, inter-UAV coordination, and ROS-based navigation logic","gap":null},"E7":{"status":"UNRESOLVED","finding":"6-page workshop paper (pp. 711-716) in the DroNet 2025 workshop co-located with ACM MobiSys '25 with substantive method and evaluation results, tagged peer-review in Macquarie University Pure (with PDF title variant Tracking vs. indexed Monitoring), but direct publisher/workshop peer-review policy pages were blocked from web browsing.","source":"https://dblp.org/db/conf/mobisys/mobisys2025","page":null,"locator":"DroNet section pp. 711-716; checked PDF p. 1, sites.google.com/view/dronet2025/, [invalid URL]","quote":"Continuous Marine Monitoring via Autonomous UAV Handoff. 711-716","gap":"ACM DL record ([invalid URL]) and DroNet 2025 submissions page (https://www.google.com/search?q=sites.google.com/view/dronet2025/submissions) were inaccessible, leaving direct publisher/workshop peer-review confirmation open.","extra_evidence":[{"source":"https://researchers.mq.edu.au/en/publications/continuous-marine-monitoring-via-autonomous-uav-handoff/","page":null,"locator":"Research output type","quote":"Chapter in Book/Report/Conference proceeding › Conference proceeding contribution › peer-review"}]}},"recommendation":"EXCLUDE","primary_code":"E3","author_check":"Confirm E3 exclusion for sequential low-battery tracking handover via non-neural ORB template matching between independently running OSTrack UAVs; verify DroNet 2025 / ACM DL peer-review policy for E7 only if overriding E3."}
